her risk settings for which alberta  health services is providing that full  public health support  do you have a  follow-up yes i do thank you very much  someone on social media indicated that  at villa characters last week that the  staff were still wearing medical masks  rather than n95  given that dr teresa tam as well as the  cdc have recommended n95 in high risk  settings would you concur that perhaps  n95 would be a better option for those  staff members  we have updated our  requirements for continuing care to  indicate that when  caring for an individual with a probable  or confirmed  case of covid  that staff in addition to all of the  other ppe masks do not work by  themselves so ppe does need to include  other aspects such as a face shield  and gown and gloves in addition to that  kn 95 or n95 mask would be considered  the minimum requirement for those  settings in general when someone is  working in those settings and not  providing care to an individual who is a  suspect or confirmed case they would of  course need to always do a point of care  risk assessment as is required for all  settings but in general the minimum  requirement for those settings would be  a high quality well fitting medical mask  for the routine continuous masking and  again the n95 and kn 95  would be the requirement for settings  when dealing with a suspect or confirmed  covet case  operator can you put through the next  caller please  brianna carson smith global  hi dr hinsha i wanted to ask you about  hospitalizations  uh and dr bernie used op-ed that was  posted today she said that albert health  services is increasing icu capacity can  you tell me what that looks like how  many beds uh who will staff those beds  and are there plans to ask other  provinces or the military for help  i don't have details about alberta  health services acute care planning so  it probably would be best to direct  those specific questions to alberta  health services  it is clear that  to date our 