immunocompromising conditions  given this providing a fourth dose to  these individuals is equivalent to a  third dose for others there are  approximately eighty thousand  immunocompromised albertans who were  eligible to get their third dose in  september of twenty twenty one  this means that many of them are soon  reaching the five month mark after that  third dose  both the national advisory committee on  immunization and the alberta advisory  committee on immunization have  recommended that immunocompromised  individuals age 18 and over receive a  fourth dose of covid19 vaccine  this is why beginning this thursday  january 20th we're opening up booking  opportunities for immunocompromised  adult albertans to receive a fourth dose  of covid19 vaccine at least five months  after their third  fourth doses will help those who are  immunocompromised to have the same level  of immunity as those of us who are  receiving our third dose and who are not  immunocompromised  this will not only help prevent some  breakthrough infections that we've been  seeing but also decrease the chance that  people with immune compromising  conditions could have severe outcomes  from covid19  the list of conditions eligible for a  fourth dose remain the same as those who  are eligible for a third dose due to a  clinical diagnosis or treatment in  september of 2021  you can visit alberta.ca to review that  list  to be clear fourth doses are only  recommended at this time for those who  have these specific medical conditions  and are immunocompromised  others who do not have these clinical  conditions are not eligible for forth  doses  even if they're nearing five months from  a third dose they may have received for  travel purposes  individuals with immunocompromising  conditions may still have some risk even  with this fourth dose  we must continue to work together to  ensure those most vulnerable in our  communities are protected  the current variant is highly infectious  and some may even be wondering if it's  inevitable if they will get it  i would like to remind people of the  reason that omicron appears to be milder  which is largely because of vaccines  vaccines dramatically reduce the risk of  severe outcomes even as we have seen  some waning of protection against  infection  even with vaccines getting sick with  covet is not a common cold  you cannot predict how far your  infection will spread through others  through an ongoing chain of transmission  or who it will reach  unfortunately at some point it will  likely reach someone for whom covet 19  is not a mild illness  so for those people i am asking all  albertans to remain vigilant  we are all connected to each other those  we know and those we don't know and the  actions we take every day make a  difference  thank you and i'm happy to take  questions  thank you dr hinshaw we'll now move to  the q a portion of the update operator  can you put through the first caller  please  yes hi there um i wanted to ask you  about billa keratin  and the explosion of cases that's  occurred there over the past nine days  115 patients and 10 staff and two deaf  what is alberta health doing to try and  curb this  outbreak and do you think enough is  being done  with respect to specific individual  outbreaks the supports for those  outbreaks are provided by alberta health  services to the organizations  so unfortunately i don't have the  details of all the individual outbreaks  that are happening right now certainly  we have seen some continuing care  outbreaks with larger numbers of cases  to date we have not seen the same impact  of severe outcomes that we've seen in  previous waves however it is early  and we continue to monitor closely if  you want specific information about how  that outbreak is being managed i would  recommend talking to either the operator  or alberta health services may have  information on the public health  supports that are being provided because  this would be one of those  higher risk settings for which alberta  health services is providing that full  public health support  do you have a  follow-up yes i do thank you very much  someone on social media indicated that  at villa characters last week that the  staff were still wearing medical masks  rather than n95  given that dr teresa tam as well as the  cdc have recommended n95 in high risk  settings would you concur that perhaps  n95 would be a better option for those  staff members  we have updated our  requirements for continuing care to  indicate that when  caring for an individual with a probable  or confirmed  case of covid  that staff in addition to all of the  other ppe masks do not work by  themselves so ppe does need to include  other aspects such as a face shield  and gown and gloves in addition to that  kn 95 or n95 mask would be considered  the minimum requirement for those  settings in general when someone is  working in those settings and not  providing care to an individual who is a  suspect or confirmed case they would of  course need to always do a point of care  risk assessment as is required for all  settings but in general the minimum  requirement for those settings would be  a high quality well fitting medical mask  for the routine continuous masking and  again the n95 and kn 95  would be the requirement for settings  when dealing with a suspect or confirmed  covet case  operator can you put through the next  caller please  brianna carson smith global  hi dr hinsha i wanted to ask you about  hospitalizations  uh and dr bernie used op-ed that was  posted today she said that albert health  services is increasing icu capacity can  you tell me what that looks like how  many beds uh who will staff those beds  and are there plans to ask other  provinces or the military for help  i don't have details about alberta  health services acute care planning so  it probably would be best to direct  those specific questions to alberta  health services  it is clear that  to date our 