very wave that  hospitalizations continue to rise after  the peak of cases and we have not uh  seen that peak of acute care impact and  again we are there there's no definitive  proof that we are currently at the peak  so we do need to continue to watch and  it's also important to remember that  half of the cases that happen in a wave  happen on the second side of the peak  which means that everything that we do  to minimize transmission  whether it's before the peak or  immediately after still matters and it  matters until that transmission has  dropped to very low levels so our  actions are important  again whether or not we reach the peak  in the in the next week or so  jason do you have a follow-up  yeah thanks um i'm wondering what the  current number of healthcare workers is  who are off due to covid19 illness or  exposure and what impact it's having in  patient care and if you're unable to  answer the question since it pertains to  uh ahs acute care operations  i'm wondering if the province will  commit to making dr verney you available  on upcoming covid19 press conferences to  seal these types of questions  an important question and you're right i  don't have that information as it is  more operational um i also am not part  of the conversations about who attends  these press conferences uh but certainly  that question  is something that our communications  team can consider but again  unfortunately i i personally don't have  the ability to make those decisions  operator please put through the next  caller  lauren poland global calgary  hi dr hinshaw just following up on some  of these hospitalization related  questions i do understand that  a lot of them are under ahs protocol but  we are at a point right now  where uh the situation is actually less  people in hospital a little more in icu  than when we saw these levers pulled  last time of redeployment of postponing  surgeries and procedures  again i understand that you don't have  it on hand  but any information for albertans on if  we could see this happen could surgeries  be postponed could procedures be  postponed and and how are we going to  staff this if we do get to that level  unfortunately again that the details of  staffing aren't things i could comment  on but it is absolutely clear that as  the burden of acute care  rises with respect to coveted missions  that postponement of surgeries or other  procedures that are non-urgent  is absolutely something that  is possible as the system makes space  for the need to care for patients who  who have covid and again even those who  are admitted for other reasons  but have a covet infection  need to have additional protocols to  prevent spread of infection so there are  still impacts in terms of capacity even  with those patients so again  absolutely there could be an impact on  surgeries and other procedures  the details unfortunately would have to  be something ahs could comment on  lauren do you have a follow-up  i do thank you  we are now uh in the fifth wave of this  uh we have hit record hospitalizations  during the fourth wave as well can you  explain to me how did we get to this  point again and do you take any  responsibility for what led us here  i think that one of the major factors is  the high transmissibility of the omicron  variant  the question about the progression of  the wave certainly in every wave we've  had  we've seen  a  series of  points in time where the situation is  assessed  i have provided  recommendations along the way  and so the the decisions about  managing cases managing the wave i can't  speak  on behalf of elected officials about  decisions all i can say is that  omicron is more challenging than  anything we have dealt with before that  it is more transmissible it does cause  more breakthrough infections  and that  the decisions about managing that  pressure again are not mine to make  operator please put through the next  caller  dylan short post media  hi dr hinshaw looking a